    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How physically fit do I need to be for the Gokyo Lakes trek?",
        "answer": "You need good cardiovascular fitness and leg endurance. You should be able to comfortably walk 5 to 7 hours uphill on consecutive days carrying a 5 to 6 kg daypack. A dedicated 8- to 12-week training routine involving stair climbing, jogging, and weighted hill walks is strongly recommended."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is Gokyo Lakes harder than Everest Base Camp?",
        "answer": "Gokyo Lakes has steeper daily inclines—particularly between Mong La, Dole, and the 31% gradient up Gokyo Ri. However, Everest Base Camp is slightly harder overall because you sleep at extreme altitude (Gorak Shep at 5,164 m) and walk roughly 38 kilometers further."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the highest point on the Gokyo Lakes trek?",
        "answer": "The highest point on the standard trek is the summit of Gokyo Ri at 5,357 meters (17,575 ft). The highest sleeping altitude is Gokyo village at 4,790 meters (15,715 ft)."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I need mountaineering skills or climbing equipment?",
        "answer": "No technical mountaineering skills, ropes, ice axes, or harnesses are required for the standard Gokyo Lakes trek. It is a non-technical trekking peak route. However, microspikes are highly recommended for icy morning patches and high pass crossings."
      },
      {
        "question": "How common is altitude sickness on the Gokyo trek?",
        "answer": "Mild symptoms of Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS), such as mild headache or shortness of breath, affect 40% to 50% of trekkers above 3,500 meters. Following a strict acclimatization itinerary with a rest day in Namche Bazaar and drinking 3 to 4 liters of water daily drastically reduces severe risks."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can beginners do the Gokyo Lakes trek?",
        "answer": "Yes, enthusiastic beginners with good general fitness and mental determination can complete the trek successfully. Having a licensed Sherpa guide to pace your ascent, monitor blood oxygen levels, and manage teahouse bookings makes a critical difference."
      },
      {
        "question": "How steep is the climb up Gokyo Ri?",
        "answer": "Gokyo Ri is exceptionally steep. It gains 567 vertical meters over just 1.8 horizontal kilometers, giving an average gradient of over 31% on loose gravel and scree switchbacks. It takes 2.5 to 3.5 hours of slow, rhythmic stepping to reach the summit."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I do if I get sick with AMS above Dole?",
        "answer": "The definitive treatment for altitude sickness is immediate descent. If symptoms do not improve with rest and fluids, descending 400 to 600 meters to Phortse Tenga or Namche Bazaar quickly relieves hypoxia. In life-threatening emergencies, helicopter evacuation is coordinated by your guide."
      }
    ],
