        "id": "the-everest-exception-explained",
        "h2": "3. The Everest (Khumbu) Exception: How Local Autonomy Affects EBC",
        "content": "\n        <p>\n          Why is the Everest region treated differently? Under Nepal's federal constitution, local rural municipalities possess substantial administrative autonomy.\n        </p>\n        <p>\n          When the central Nepal Tourism Board announced the blanket solo trekking ban in 2023, the <strong>Khumbu Pasang Lhamu Rural Municipality</strong> exercised its constitutional power:\n        </p>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Local Entry Permit Independence:</strong> The Khumbu municipality abolished the central TIMS card within its territory and established its own direct local entry fee (NPR 3,000 / ~$25 USD), collected at Lukla and Monjo.</li>\n          <li><strong>Current Trail Reality in 2026:</strong> Because the Khumbu local government relies on direct tourist revenue, they have permitted independent foreign hikers who purchase the local permit and Sagarmatha National Park entry to walk the primary, highly populated highway from Lukla to Namche Bazaar and Everest Base Camp.</li>\n          <li><strong>Why You Should Still Hire a Guide for Everest:</strong> While technically possible to walk solo on the main EBC route, hiring a guide remains essential:\n            <ul>\n              <li>Managing severe altitude (EBC sleeps at 5,164m; Kala Patthar rises to 5,545m).</li>\n              <li>Navigating technical boulder moraines and glacial crossings on Cho La, Kongma La, and Renjo La passes.</li>\n              <li>Securing scarce teahouse rooms in Lobuche and Gorak Shep during peak season.</li>\n            </ul>\n          </li>\n        </ul>\n      "
