        "id": "the-tims-card-overhaul-how-system-works",
        "h2": "5. The TIMS Card Overhaul: How Guide Booking Is Linked to Permits",
        "content": "\n        <p>\n          Prior to the mandate, foreign travelers could walk into the Nepal Tourism Board offices in Bhrikutimandap (Kathmandu) or Damside (Pokhara), pay NPR 2,000, and receive a green \"Free Independent Trekker (FIT)\" TIMS card.\n        </p>\n        <p>\n          <strong>That system is completely abolished in 2026:</strong>\n        </p>\n        <ol>\n          <li><strong>Green Solo TIMS Cards Discontinued:</strong> The Nepal Tourism Board stopped printing and issuing individual green TIMS cards.</li>\n          <li><strong>Exclusive Agency Distribution:</strong> All TIMS cards (now issued digitally with QR tracking codes) are exclusively accessible to authorized, TAAN-registered trekking agencies.</li>\n          <li><strong>Mandatory Guide ID Linking:</strong> To generate a digital TIMS card for any foreign trekker, the agency must input the specific government license number and insurance policy details of the assigned guide into the centralized national database.</li>\n          <li><strong>Digital Checkpost Validation:</strong> Trail checkpost officers scan the QR code on the traveler's digital TIMS document. If no registered guide accompanies the party, entry is denied.</li>\n        </ol>\n      "
