        "content": "\n        <figure class=\"article-figure\">\n          <img src=\"../images/licensed-trekking-guide-leading-foreign-trekkers-on-a-himalayan-trail-in-nepal.webp\" alt=\"A professional licensed trekking guide in an official windbreaker leading foreign trekkers on a Himalayan ridge trail in Nepal\" class=\"article-figure-img\" width=\"1200\" height=\"700\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n          <figcaption class=\"article-figure-caption\">\n            Certified leadership: A legal mountain guide carries a Department of Tourism photo ID card, graduated from NATHM, and possesses active medical first aid certification.\n          </figcaption>\n        </figure>\n        <p>\n          To satisfy the legal requirement, your companion cannot simply be a local friend, a hotel receptionist, or an informal porter. They must hold an <strong>official government license</strong>:\n        </p>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>NATHM Certification:</strong> Legitimate trekking guides must graduate from the rigorous 5-week professional curriculum at the <strong>Nepal Academy of Tourism and Hotel Management (NATHM)</strong>, covering alpine navigation, mountain ecology, altitude medicine, rescue protocols, and foreign languages.</li>\n          <li><strong>Department of Tourism License Card:</strong> Upon graduation, the Department of Tourism issues a photo ID card with a unique 4- or 5-digit license number. This license must be renewed annually with proof of active tax compliance.</li>\n          <li><strong>Why Freelance Street Guides Are Risky:</strong> Unregistered freelancers offering guide services in Thamel or Lakeside cannot generate valid TIMS cards. If checked at a park gate, you will be stopped, and both you and the unauthorized guide will face official questioning and fines.</li>\n        </ul>\n      "
